WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.340
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.710
I'm very excited to announce
our last and final keynote,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.930
Jeff Leek. We are
particularly lucky to get

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:23.290
Jeff Leek as a keynote
speaker right now because he's

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:25.090
in the middle of a
cross-country move

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:28.720
to start a new position as
VP and Chief data officer

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.625
at the Fred Hutchinson
Institute for Cancer Research.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.690
So Jeff is famous for very many
things, but two in particular

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:45.900
that I wanted to talk about.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.040
The first is along with
Roger Peng and Brian Caffo,

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.710
Jeff taught really, like,
the first data science

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:56.240
MOOC which introduced R
to literally millions of people,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.310
which is pretty amazing.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.600
The other thing
I love about Jeff

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.750
is that him and I
disagree on a few things.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.700
And one of the
things in particular,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.330
several years ago he wrote a
blog post why I don't use ggplot2,

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.880
but what I love
about Jeff's writing

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.810
is he articulates his position

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:22.410
so clearly,

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.170
like, even as I was
reading the article,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.100
I was like nodding along,
yeah, yeah, that makes sense.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.720
So please join me in
welcoming Jeff Leek.

00:01:37.712 --> 00:01:38.420
Thanks very much.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:38.960
And thanks, Hadley.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.020
I know you've been saving
that up for a minute.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.190
I will say that I have become
a ggplot2 convert since then.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.160
So yes, Yes.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.210
I'm not even playing
to the audience there.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.687
Yeah so I'm very
excited to be here today

00:01:52.687 --> 00:01:55.270
to talk to you about DataTrail,
which is an effort that we've

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.120
been running in Baltimore for
the last four or five years

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.030
to build inclusive, community
based data science education.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.560
And I hope that you'll
be excited to go back

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.160
into your communities and also
build programs and connect

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.378
to your communities.

00:02:10.378 --> 00:02:11.920
One thing I wanted
to start off with,

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.670
though, is I wanted to just say
I'm so excited to be in a room

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:16.640
with all of you.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.400
I've seen tons of
people that I haven't

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.440
seen for 2 and 1/2 years.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.370
The very last conference
I went to in January 2020,

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.860
before this one was
RStudio conf in San Francisco.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.060
I then got on an airplane,
flew back to Baltimore

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.850
and the world ended.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.970
I know that all of us over
the last 2 and 1/2 years

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.250
have gone through
many, many things,

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.600
they're both individual
and collective.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.180
And I just want
to express the joy

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.055
that I feel to be back
together with people

00:02:45.055 --> 00:02:46.930
and back with the R
community, and especially

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.870
this amazing community that
the band formerly called

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.030
RStudio has put together.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.280
And so I'm really
excited about that.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.160
And I just wanted to
express my gratitude

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.360
both for the invitation
to speak here today.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.750
And to the community that's
been built, all of you,

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.050
to coming out and sticking
around for the last talk.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.450
Before I go any further,
I also wanted to make sure

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:11.740
I Thank the people that did
all the real important work.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:15.730
This is the DataTrail
core team, Ashley Johnson,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:20.020
Simone Sawyer, Ed Sabatino,
Davon Person. And Ashley

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.500
and Davon are
actually here today.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.920
And if they could stand up
and wave there somewhere

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.490
in the audience, there
they are right there.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:29.420
Anything good you hear,

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.470
anything good hear is
definitely because of them.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:36.877
Anything silly or
misguided is due to me.

00:03:36.877 --> 00:03:38.710
This is a kind of project
that can't be done

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:40.100
by just a couple of people.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.800
There's been a huge
community of people

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.240
that have helped
us over the years

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:44.750
develop the DataTrail program.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.160
And I wanted to say that we're
so grateful to all the people

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.172
that helped out.

00:03:50.172 --> 00:03:52.380
One of the things I wanted
to talk a little bit about

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.580
is my own journey to
where I am here today.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.580
As Hadley mentioned, I'm
in the middle of a move

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.070
to take a big, exciting new
job right near my family,

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.600
which I'm really excited about.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.190
But I didn't start
there, as none of us did.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:06.210
I started off as just a nerdy
kid from a very small town

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.440
in Idaho.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.840
This is an embarrassing picture
that usually other people

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.450
show in a presentation,
but how did that happen?

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:17.050
How did I go from nerdy kid
in Idaho to where I am today?

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:20.829
And I imagine we all had a
similar kind of journey, right?

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:23.370
We had to know that this was
a field which some are just

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.220
discovering now.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:25.710
Some discovered a
long time ago, we

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.877
had to have access to the
equipment and the training

00:04:27.877 --> 00:04:31.680
and the connections to be able
to be in a room here today.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.910
But more importantly than that,
we had I had to have help.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.240
This is not something
that I did on my own.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.090
I had to be lifted
up at various points

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.340
of my career from my
undergrad advisor, Jim Powell,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.370
to my grad advisor John Storey,
to Giovanni Parmigiani, who

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:45.870
recruited me to
Baltimore by saying,

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.328
do you want to live
near your wife?

00:04:47.328 --> 00:04:51.552
To Rafa Irizarry, who
was my main faculty

00:04:51.552 --> 00:04:53.010
mentor in my early
years, and many,

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.787
many other people who have
worked with me just like me.

00:04:56.787 --> 00:04:59.370
I'm sure you feel the same way
that there are people that have

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:00.820
helped you out in your life.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.700
And so I wanted to
just take a minute

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.040
because we're all here together
to turn to your neighbor

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.050
and maybe tell
them about somebody

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:08.520
that helped you along your way.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.720
We're going to just
take a minute here,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.250
and I'd like you to
tell somebody about one

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.500
of your mentors, somebody
who helped you out when

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.650
you had a moment of need.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:15.990
So I'm just going to
give a brief pause

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:17.320
and make it a
little interactive.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.528
Please talk to your neighbor.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.955
If you're online on discord,
you can talk the same way.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:35.386
This is awesome.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.130
All right.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:25.760
I know that this conversation
could last a long time.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.000
And I hope you'll continue
that conversation later.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.580
And I hope it reminded you
of somebody that helped you

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.730
along the way, because the
DataTrail program we're building

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:36.710
is about helping other people.

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:39.170
And so one of the key
things that we realize

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.150
that I think
everybody recognizes

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:43.580
is that talent is
equally distributed.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.980
So these are some young
people from our second cohort

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:49.580
of DataTrail scholars
in East Baltimore.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.980
These are folks that
grew up around there

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.190
and are incredibly
talented young people.

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:59.000
And there's an important
thing that even

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.010
though the talent is
equally distributed,

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.150
opportunity isn't always.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.510
So this is data from the
economic opportunity atlas,

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.490
and it says that if you
were born and grew up

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.850
in this neighborhood, the median
family income when you get

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:13.790
to age 34 is $18,000 a year.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:16.340
So that whole neighborhood,
even though it's

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.160
right next to a multibillion
dollar medical institution,

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:20.810
doesn't have access
to the same career

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:24.642
opportunities that say I did
when I grew up out in Idaho.

00:07:24.642 --> 00:07:26.600
And it's not just Baltimore
where this is true.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.630
If you look at the
economic opportunity atlas,

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:34.300
where I'm going in Seattle,
Pittsburgh, Chicago, Tampa Bay,

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.610
New York city, no
matter where you look,

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.720
there are these
opportunity deserts.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.450
And so how do we
create opportunity

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.050
in these opportunity deserts?

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:44.530
How do we actively go out
and promote opportunities

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.480
for folks that grew up there?

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.340
And it's not just economic.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.060
It's not just an
economic calculation.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:51.590
It's also a health calculation.

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:53.090
I was in a school
of Public Health.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.410
I'm moving to a Cancer Center.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:55.900
I care a lot about health.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:56.960
This is a plot.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.940
It doesn't look like real
data, but it is real data.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.780
This is a plot of income
percentile on the x-axis.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.900
And on the y-axis
is life expectancy.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.370
You've got one line for
female, one line for male.

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:10.210
And you can see that there's
near-perfect correlation

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.040
between how much money
your parents made

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.220
and your life expectancy on
average in the population,

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.510
which is kind of incredible.

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:19.470
Education is still
the best treatment

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:23.540
we have to cure the problem
of lack of opportunity.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.850
This is a plot that shows
how much your parents made

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:27.210
on the x-axis.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.360
And on the y-axis
is how much you

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.039
make after going to
different types of schools.

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:34.250
Each color represents a
different type of school

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:36.169
and the lines are
relatively flat,

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:38.990
which means if you get
into one of those schools,

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:43.010
you have a very good chance
of moving up economically.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.390
But the challenge is
you can't always get in.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:47.800
So this is a similar plot.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.310
This is college's,
each dot is a college.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.440
On the x-axis is how likely you
are to get into that college

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.370
on a scale from 0 to 100.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:58.300
If you're a parent, based
on your parent's income

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.280
and on the y-axis
is how likely you

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:04.510
are to be in the top
quartile of income

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.170
after completing college there.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.350
So places like Johns Hopkins,
where I used to be at,

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:09.225
have very low access.

00:09:09.225 --> 00:09:11.017
If your parents didn't
have a lot of money,

00:09:11.017 --> 00:09:13.220
it's unlikely you're going
to get into that college.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:14.800
But if you go
there, you're going

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.125
to have a very good chance of
having a high economic return.

00:09:18.125 --> 00:09:19.500
And so you can
actually calculate

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:20.458
this for every college.

00:09:20.458 --> 00:09:22.943
You can say, multiply the
probability you get in.

00:09:22.943 --> 00:09:24.360
If you're from the
lowest quintile

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.180
of income times the probability
you'll be in the highest income

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.570
if you get it once you get out.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.640
And that can be what
we call mobility rate.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.790
This is a calculation
developed by some economists.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:37.210
And so the best generators of
mobility in the United States

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:40.660
are typically large public
institutions, community

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.260
colleges, technical
colleges that

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:47.320
produce the highest level
of economic mobility

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:48.460
in our country.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.270
And they're driven by access.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:52.270
So you can see that the
success rates don't have

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:53.450
to be in the high 90s to be.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:55.450
These are the very
top colleges in the US

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:59.555
and it's driven by how
accessible that education is.

00:09:59.555 --> 00:10:01.680
And so we're all here
because we love data science.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.260
We love R, we love Python, we
love all the other languages.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:09.980
And it's a high growth
and wage job category.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:12.873
And so how do we get more
people to have access to this?

00:10:12.873 --> 00:10:14.540
As Hadley mentioned,
we've been teaching

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.610
massive online open
courses about data science

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:19.640
for a long time, and they've now
enrolled more than 10 million

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:22.630
people across the world.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.650
And these programs can
lead to big income gains.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:28.360
So here I'm showing
a plot on the x-axis

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.900
is the cost of the program.

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:32.680
And I've got graduate school,
undergraduate, associate's

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.020
degree, and on the
y-axis is the increase

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.610
in income post program.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.610
And for one of our
data science sequences,

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:41.980
you get nearly the same
amount of income boost

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:44.380
as you would for a college
or advanced degree,

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.400
even for a much lower cost.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:47.740
So that seems pretty exciting.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:49.240
And originally we
were thinking, OK,

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:52.860
this is how we generate
this mobility at scale.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.500
But as, not just us,
but many, many people

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.710
have noted Coursera
classes, bootcamps,

00:10:58.710 --> 00:10:59.890
those sorts of things.

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:02.430
They tend to benefit the
already well educated.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:05.760
Most of the people taking these
programs are actually already

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.530
have bachelor's degrees,
master's degrees,

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:10.020
professional degrees in
a career transitioning.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.650
So we're not really generating
access for the people

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.050
the mobility rate applies to.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.090
And so what we try
to do is, can we

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.640
create opportunities
for the folks that

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.290
aren't taking the
massive online open

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.360
courses who haven't
been introduced to R

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.170
via these scalable programs?

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:29.395
And lest I say that we're
the only people thinking

00:11:29.395 --> 00:11:31.020
about this, there
are a ton of amazing.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.687
I've been to talks
today from Openscapes

00:11:32.687 --> 00:11:35.700
to you met amazing people from
various parts of the world.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.700
We've got R-Ladies,
we've got Data Carpentry,

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:40.520
the Bioconductor team, so
many people that are also

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:42.433
trying to do this to
try to lift people up

00:11:42.433 --> 00:11:44.600
through the power of data
science education and data

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.810
science opportunities.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.020
But the reality is there are
significant barriers to entry.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.960
I showed this graph a
minute ago for myself,

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.830
but it applies to anyone who
wants to get into data science.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:57.380
You have to know
about data science.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.547
You have to have the income
security to participate.

00:11:59.547 --> 00:12:03.010
You have to have access to a
computer, programs, instruction,

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.840
connections, the right jobs.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:06.820
It's a lot.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:10.120
So how do we take these
barriers down one by one?

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.292
So first you have to
know about data science.

00:12:12.292 --> 00:12:13.750
And so the way that
we've addressed

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:15.728
that problem with
our program is we

00:12:15.728 --> 00:12:17.770
actually go out into the
community and we partner

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:21.010
with community based education
associations like historic East

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:25.030
Baltimore Community Action
Coalition and the HeartSmiles,

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:28.810
who train young people to get
their high school diplomas,

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:30.770
who train them to get
technical training.

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:33.430
And we teach them
about the opportunities

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:35.140
that are there in data science.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:39.660
We partner with them to recruit
students for our program.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.587
Then how do we
address the computer?

00:12:41.587 --> 00:12:44.170
So this is the thing that maybe
kicked off all of this for me.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:48.188
I love Chromebooks and you
can get a Chromebook for $450.

00:12:48.188 --> 00:12:49.980
We've seen the power
of this with education

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:52.410
over the last couple of
years with the pandemic.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.900
And it turns out that
you can be a data

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.380
scientist on a Chromebook.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.957
And this is where,
again RStudio and Posit

00:12:58.957 --> 00:13:00.540
have been a major
player in what we've

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:02.915
been able to do by
creating RStudio Cloud.

00:13:02.915 --> 00:13:04.290
We were one of
the first programs

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:05.950
to jump on to that platform.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:07.560
So that you can do
all of your data

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:09.660
science work directly
on the cloud.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.160
It's such an
incredible tool and I'm

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:12.785
so grateful that
we've been able to use

00:13:12.785 --> 00:13:14.680
that to help train people.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:17.080
And so you can be a data
scientist on a chromebook,

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:19.090
you can use Google
Slides and Google Sheets

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:22.490
and you can use RStudio and you
can teach people how to do it.

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:26.770
And they don't need a MacBook
Pro to be a data scientist.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:28.660
There's also barriers to entry.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:30.730
You know, if you need
to buy a MacBook pro,

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.090
it costs you $3,000.

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:34.600
If you only if your family
only made $18,000 last year,

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:37.330
you can't participate
in a program like this.

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:39.070
So with funding
from Johns Hopkins

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:41.530
originally, later from
the Abell Foundation,

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:43.970
we actually pay the students
to complete the courses.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.120
So as they finish our
massive online open courses,

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.210
they earn money so that they can
participate in it like a part

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.530
time job.

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:52.030
Thank you.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:53.000
Yeah Thanks to Abell.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:54.000
Thanks to Johns Hopkins.

00:13:57.698 --> 00:13:59.240
We also need access
to the right kind

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:02.030
of instruction and the
appropriate programs.

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:04.740
So, here's where
R really shines.

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:06.147
Why would we use R?

00:14:06.147 --> 00:14:07.730
I get asked this
question all the time

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:10.378
when we built our entire
training program around R.

00:14:10.378 --> 00:14:12.170
I know that there's
also Python people here

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:14.420
and we love Python too, but
we built our whole program

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:17.060
around R and I get asked
a lot why we did that.

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:20.990
And the reason why is we wanted
to minimize the time to magic.

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:23.420
So with just a few lines
of code, R Markdown,

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:25.580
with just a few lines of
code can put a website up

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:28.190
on the web that's interactive
that you can show off

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:30.900
to your friends
and to your family.

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:34.780
With things like the bigrquery
package and dbplyr

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:36.810
you can connect to
massive databases

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:38.940
and analyze huge
collections of data

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.490
with just a few lines of code.

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:44.130
And with things like
Shiny, flexdashboard,

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.590
Shiny dashboards,
those, you can also

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:48.100
write just a few
lines of code. Here

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:49.920
it's like 34 lines
of code that creates

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.700
an interactive
NHANES website.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:55.280
So with minimizing
time to magic,

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:59.000
we can train people very
quickly to do incredible things.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.810
And then we can build
a program around it.

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:02.270
We can build a
community around it

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:04.460
that helps support
people as they go through

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:06.650
and they learn how
to do data science.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.070
We do this on Basecamp.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:10.790
You can also do it on Slack or
any other community building

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.710
platform, but there
has to be both

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:16.320
an online and
in-person component.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.240
So then the last
part is you have

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.528
to think about, OK, say we
have a training program.

00:15:20.528 --> 00:15:21.820
So we've trained them to do it.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:24.850
We've built it in R. Then they
need access to connections.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.840
They need to be able
to actually get a job.

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:29.580
And so here we can
again, leverage the

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:32.760
R community, the people
here in the audience today

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:35.040
to help make connections
between the young people

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:36.990
that we're training and
the broader community

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:39.490
and opportunities
that are out there.

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:41.450
And this ecosystem really works.

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:43.658
We've had 80% success rate
in our Johns Hopkins pilot

00:15:43.658 --> 00:15:45.283
that I'm going to
tell you a little bit

00:15:45.283 --> 00:15:48.230
about completing the training.
Just to give you some idea,

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:50.340
massive online open
courses in general -

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:53.870
the completion rate hovers
somewhere between 2% and 5%.

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:57.580
So 80% completion rate for this
massive online open sequence

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:00.853
is an incredible testament
to the will and intelligence

00:16:00.853 --> 00:16:03.020
of the people that we're
training in the community.

00:16:10.323 --> 00:16:12.490
After they, after they
finished the training program,

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.615
though, we want to put them
into opportunities that

00:16:14.615 --> 00:16:16.540
can help change their lives.

00:16:16.540 --> 00:16:19.330
And so their reality is there
are significant barriers

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:20.660
to entry to these jobs.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.380
But we've partnered
with programs

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:27.850
that support internships that
represent a soft landing place.

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:30.560
We teach people data
science in 16 weeks.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.650
That is not a lot of time
to learn data science

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:34.610
and learn how to be
a data scientist.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:36.970
So we partner with programs
like Urban Alliance,

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:38.500
and we have a couple
of people here,

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:40.310
Chaz and LaShonda
from Urban Alliance.

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:41.768
I don't know where
they're sitting,

00:16:41.768 --> 00:16:44.080
but they're also in the
audience here today, over there.

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:46.510
And they're helping us build
internship programs that

00:16:46.510 --> 00:16:49.120
support the long component
of training that's

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:51.970
developing the soft skills,
developing the approach

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:56.130
to work that will let them be
in these careers long term.

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.350
So I'll tell you a little
bit now about our pilot

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:00.780
that we ran in Baltimore.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:03.360
This is what the whole
pilot has felt like.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.230
We started this off with just an
idea and absolutely no program.

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:11.730
And so at every turn, it
felt like we're barely

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.200
getting the train
tracks together

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:17.609
in time to be able to
continue to run the program.

00:17:17.609 --> 00:17:19.119
So this is how it started.

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:22.950
In February 2018, we started
developing content for our data

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:24.420
science training program.

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:27.150
We met with HEBCAC, Historic
East Baltimore Community Action

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:29.877
Coalition, in April of
2018, and we told them

00:17:29.877 --> 00:17:32.460
we were developing a program and
we were excited about working

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:33.360
with them.

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:35.992
They got very excited and they
said, can we start tomorrow?

00:17:35.992 --> 00:17:37.950
Remember, we had started
developing our program

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:38.900
in February.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.720
It was April.

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:43.250
And so they said, OK, we'll
give you a little time.

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:46.100
We'll start in May.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:49.310
We had to develop this much
content between February

00:17:49.310 --> 00:17:50.210
and May.

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:53.150
So we're talking 12
courses to cover everything

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:57.200
from how to use computers to
how do you organize projects

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:00.290
to the tidyverse to
written an oral communication

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:05.570
to how to get a job. 12
courses in five months.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:06.770
So this was me.

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:08.280
I was a little stressed.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:10.750
I was worried that we
wouldn't be able to do it.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.695
But fortunately we had some
really incredible help.

00:18:13.695 --> 00:18:15.820
So at the time, there are
two people I particularly

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.400
want to call out Shannon
Ellis and Aboozar Hadavand,

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:19.950
who are postdocs in my group.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.330
One was an economist
working on studying

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:25.070
how the outcomes of these
massive online open courses.

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:26.890
And one was a geneticist.

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:28.450
But they pitched
in and they said,

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.510
we can build this whole
program in that much time.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.460
So they assembled this
collaborative team

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:35.560
and on github,
using R Markdown,

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:38.640
we built this entire
program in that much time.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.390
We did have some
challenges along the way,

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:43.120
though. As we were
building the program,

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:45.610
the courses like getting
and cleaning data

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:49.660
were being updated because
new packages would come out.

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:53.140
So this is a plot.
On the x-axis is time.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.720
Thousands of R scripts after
the start of a program

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:59.250
and on the y-axis is the
usage of different packages.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:01.060
There's one that
kind of stands out,

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:03.685
and that's because right in the
middle of when we're developing

00:19:03.685 --> 00:19:06.460
these courses, dplyr comes out
rendering half of our program

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.520
totally valueless, and we
have to start from scratch.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:10.230
Thanks, Hadley.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:16.400
So what we had to do is
regenerate all of our materials

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:18.470
and we now pay very
careful attention

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:20.930
to what the RStudio
and Posit folks

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:24.870
are doing on their GitHub repos
as we're developing courses.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:26.080
We built this program.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:28.260
And so now we were ready
to start training people.

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:30.340
So we thought, well,
let's start May 1st.

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:32.400
How long does the program last?

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:33.450
They asked me.

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:34.570
We'd never run it before.

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:36.660
In fact, we had just
built the program.

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:38.500
I don't know how long
the program lasts.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:40.350
Let's call it 12 weeks.

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:42.550
So I told them we were
going to do it in 12 weeks.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:43.690
Shannon, Aboozar, and I

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:46.950
said, OK, we're going
to finish it by August 31.

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:49.200
Despite us still having
to fix up courses,

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.720
all the bugs, our
two pilot candidates

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:53.680
finished in about 14 weeks.

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:54.220
16 weeks, sorry.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:56.730
So that was
pretty incredible.

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:58.840
We took two young
people from HEBCAC.

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:00.780
They'd never heard of
data science before.

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:03.150
We got them through an
entire 12 course MOOC

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:04.720
sequence they both finished.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:06.730
It was awesome.

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:09.190
Now they'd finish the data
science training program,

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:12.620
and we realized we
got to get them a job.

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:14.120
And so there's a
bit of a challenge

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:16.400
that we ran into that you
may have run into sometimes

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:18.350
in your search for jobs.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:20.120
This is the roles
and responsibilities

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:23.270
you may experience that you need
to do once you get on the job.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:27.360
And this might be the job
experience they're asking for.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:28.640
We saw that a lot.

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:30.540
We saw that there
were jobs that we

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:32.550
knew that our
graduates could perform,

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.850
but were not necessarily
advertised in the right way.

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:38.520
Moreover, we ran into the
Fair Labor and Standards Act,

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:40.710
and I learned more about
the legal requirements

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:43.420
around employment than
I would care to repeat.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:46.230
But the basic idea is
that because they're not

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:48.570
computer scientists,
they actually write text

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:50.370
and they write prose
and things like that.

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:52.980
They don't qualify to be
exempt employees unless they

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:54.300
have a bachelor's degree.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:57.390
So we need them to be
hourly data scientist jobs,

00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:01.550
which it turns out a few
companies have, but many don't.

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:04.580
So with this close, we
train these young people,

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:05.460
they're brilliant.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:07.460
They finish the program,
but we can't quite

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:09.547
get them in to the finish line.

00:21:09.547 --> 00:21:11.130
So what are we going
to do about this?

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:13.213
How are we going to actually
get them to the point

00:21:13.213 --> 00:21:14.930
where they can be
employed and they

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:18.010
can move to a different
place in their life?

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:19.485
So I did what anyone would do.

00:21:19.485 --> 00:21:21.610
I called a friend of mine,
an old family friend who

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:24.760
was a consultant for IBM, and I
said, let's start a consulting

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:25.600
company.

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:28.600
And so he and I started
a consulting company,

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:31.420
and we would do data science
consulting work for companies

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:32.890
all around the country.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:34.900
We would do them for
Fortune 500 companies,

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:37.430
for small companies,
small mom and pop shops.

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:39.880
And all of the data science
work that was being done

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:41.830
was being done by
graduates of our program

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:44.950
that we were hiring from
the training program.

00:21:44.950 --> 00:21:46.280
This worked great.

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:48.573
The graduates did
an amazing job.

00:21:48.573 --> 00:21:49.990
We were able to
do it, but we were

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:53.480
a bootstrapped, bespoke data
science consulting company.

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:55.240
And ultimately,
that's not the way

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:58.980
that this is going to
work going forward.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.278
So the next part
of our story is we

00:22:01.278 --> 00:22:02.820
brought on Ashley
Johnson, who's here

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:04.237
today as a core
member of our team

00:22:04.237 --> 00:22:06.660
and is the program
manager for DataTrail.

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:11.080
And she managed through a lot
of hard work, blood, sweat,

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:14.730
and tears to get our HR
systems at Johns Hopkins, which

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:18.210
is a massive organization that
where HR does not move quickly

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:20.170
to create a new job code.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:22.200
So that we could hire
people at Johns Hopkins,

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:24.870
specifically with the
credentials from our program.

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:27.270
There's an incredible
testament to Ashley's patience

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:29.290
and brilliance getting
this through the system.

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:31.710
And it allowed us to
hire Davon Person, who's

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:34.410
a graduate of our program,
to be the main TA for all

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:37.050
of our future training cohorts.

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:39.600
This kind of work has
to be done by advocates

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:41.000
within an organization.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:42.590
This isn't data science work.

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:45.420
This is the hard
blocking and tackling

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.030
that needs to be done by
each and every one of us

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:50.040
if we want to build new
opportunities where there

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.430
weren't opportunities before.

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:53.123
Build new bridges.

00:22:53.123 --> 00:22:54.540
We have this phrase
that we always

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:56.820
say when we're building
this program hard things are

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:59.130
hard to do when you want
to make a big change

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:00.500
in the way the world works.

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:01.668
It's not going to be easy.

00:23:01.668 --> 00:23:03.210
It's going to be
hard, and that means

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:04.470
it's going to be hard to do.

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:07.950
But it's worth doing,
even though it is hard.

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:10.500
So I think I'm going to
take another brief pause

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:12.960
and I'm going to have all of
you think about sometime you

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.900
accomplish something,
something that was really hard

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:17.730
and that you're really
proud that you did,

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:19.140
even though it
took a lot of work

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:20.370
and it was kind of painful.

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:21.380
Tell your neighbor about it.

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:22.470
We'll give you another
couple of minutes

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:23.512
and then we'll come back.

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:46.110
All right.

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:53.850
We'll jump back into it.

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:04.520
All right.

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:05.600
I'm stoked about this.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:07.583
Everybody was very
excited to tell each other

00:26:07.583 --> 00:26:08.750
about their accomplishments.

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:09.350
That's great.

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:10.840
I'm excited about it.

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:14.750
Hopefully I'll get to hear some
of them later from some of you.

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:17.288
So again, hard things are
hard, but they're worth doing,

00:26:17.288 --> 00:26:19.580
as you all just were telling
each other about something

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:21.020
that you were really proud of.

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:22.850
But it took a lot of work to do.

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:25.233
So it's exciting to be
a part of those things.

00:26:25.233 --> 00:26:26.900
It's exciting to be
and compelling to be

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:29.570
part of something that feels
like it's really important,

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:31.845
even if it takes a lot of work.

00:26:31.845 --> 00:26:33.970
I'm going to tell you a
little bit about the impact

00:26:33.970 --> 00:26:35.890
of this program
and what it's meant

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:38.910
to the people in the
community and to us.

00:26:38.910 --> 00:26:41.170
So first I wanted to mention
Davon, who's here today.

00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:43.110
Again, he's I can't
remember where he is,

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:45.180
but Davon is our lead.

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:46.035
There he is.

00:26:46.035 --> 00:26:46.535
All right.

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:52.480
Davon is our curriculum
lead for DataTrail.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:55.272
He's a graduate of the
program, an incredible guy.

00:26:55.272 --> 00:26:57.730
I knew Davon was going to be
the right guy for our program

00:26:57.730 --> 00:26:59.813
when we had a dinner at
our house for all the

00:26:59.813 --> 00:27:01.120
DataTrail trainees.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:03.540
He came to my house and
he spent the whole dinner

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:06.030
teaching my, at the
time, eight-year-old son

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:07.623
had to play chess.

00:27:07.623 --> 00:27:09.540
Unfortunately, that's
bad for me because now I

00:27:09.540 --> 00:27:11.640
get beat all the time at chess.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:14.910
But it was a great testament
to the incredible talent

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:17.620
that he has for helping
people learn how to do things.

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:20.420
So it's so great to have
him as part of the program.

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:23.180
Another incredible
part of this program

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:26.690
is that one of our graduates,
while working at Problem

00:27:26.690 --> 00:27:29.420
Forward, worked on an
HBO documentary called

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:30.320
The Slow Hustle.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:31.480
If any of you have seen it.

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:33.480
If not, you should
definitely go out and see it.

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:36.923
They did a bunch of analysis
of the historic redlining

00:27:36.923 --> 00:27:38.840
in Baltimore's community
that prevented people

00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:41.930
from buying houses in different
communities within Baltimore.

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:43.850
It's quite a famous
problem around the world,

00:27:43.850 --> 00:27:46.730
but in Baltimore, it's
particularly transparent

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:50.960
how a problem it was, and so was
credited actually in the movie

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:54.830
credits for their
analysis of the data that

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:56.140
showed this redlining.

00:27:56.140 --> 00:27:58.250
It really unbelievable
effort that was

00:27:58.250 --> 00:28:01.370
made by a DataTrail graduate.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:01.870
Thank you, yeah.

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:08.488
And so this is
an example of a quote.

00:28:08.488 --> 00:28:10.030
We collect quotes
from all the people

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:11.690
that come from the program.

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:13.642
So it says, "It gave
me a new direction

00:28:13.642 --> 00:28:15.100
and taught me you
can be different,

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:16.850
go a different route
than your parents.

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:19.010
Not always hanging on to
what society tells you,

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:20.890
you can follow
your own protocol,

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:23.800
so it just gave me a sense
like a new world or a new field

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:26.170
and a new life
that I got to see."

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:28.430
It's such an incredible
thing to be a mentor.

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:29.950
It's such an
incredible thing to be

00:28:29.950 --> 00:28:32.380
a person that makes a
connection to a new field,

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:33.980
to a new opportunity.

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:36.370
And it's been incredibly
rewarding to see

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:38.440
that happen, not just
for us, but watch

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:40.720
it scale as Davon makes
connections for people,

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:42.620
as Candice makes
connections for people.

00:28:42.620 --> 00:28:46.100
As Ashley makes
connections for people.

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:48.530
But we did learn some
lessons along the way.

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:51.020
I mentioned that we were a
train that was constantly trying

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:52.570
to take things off the track.

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:54.260
This was almost
all due to the fact

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:56.498
that I'm a biostatistician
by training.

00:28:56.498 --> 00:28:58.790
I don't know the first thing
about building a community

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:01.460
organization and had
to learn over and over

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:04.460
and over again, things that
people who work in the field

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:06.050
have known for a long time.

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:08.570
We learned those by listening,
by paying attention,

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:10.350
by spending time in
those communities.

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:13.000
But they're important
and hard lessons.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:14.980
One of the most important
things we learned

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:16.780
is that I'm here
at a data science

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:18.740
conference an R conference.

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:22.060
But data science is actually
the easy part of all of this.

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:24.950
The young people that go
through our program are bright.

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:26.590
They're good at computers.

00:29:26.590 --> 00:29:28.870
Pretty soon, they're teaching
us about data science.

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:30.290
That's not the hard part.

00:29:30.290 --> 00:29:32.140
The hard part is
everything around it.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:34.780
All the stuff that comes up
in somebody's life, you know,

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:36.340
housing falling
through, not being

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:38.710
able to get transportation,
not having the access

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:40.240
to the resources,
not having access

00:29:40.240 --> 00:29:42.370
to the connections of
people that can hook you up

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:44.070
with a job or an opportunity.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:47.260
Those are the things that really
take work to make something

00:29:47.260 --> 00:29:49.520
like this happen.

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:51.830
The other thing that we
learned is that building trust.

00:29:51.830 --> 00:29:52.980
It takes time.

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:55.127
You know, we're
working in a community.

00:29:55.127 --> 00:29:57.710
We're working you know, we come
from Johns Hopkins at the time

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.505
now from the Hutch,
you know, and we're

00:29:59.505 --> 00:30:01.880
making these connections to
community based organizations

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:04.255
who've been working in those
communities for a long time.

00:30:04.255 --> 00:30:04.980
But we're new.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:07.680
You know, we're not we're not
part of that community yet.

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:10.130
And it takes time
and it takes effort

00:30:10.130 --> 00:30:13.160
and it takes being there
all the time consistently

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:16.550
to be able to build that trust
so that you can actually build

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:18.980
a relationship that will
last and will create people

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:20.735
that move on to the next step.

00:30:20.735 --> 00:30:22.610
You know, it's hard to
tell somebody you just

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:24.440
met what's really going on.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.300
One of the things that's
always the biggest

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:28.520
challenge in our program
is getting people

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:30.020
to ask questions.

00:30:30.020 --> 00:30:32.780
It's intimidating to
ask questions when

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:34.170
you're starting something new.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:37.397
It's a scary thing to admit
you don't know something.

00:30:37.397 --> 00:30:39.230
And so it's an important
part of the program

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:41.960
to build trust, to build those
connections so that people can

00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:44.600
comfortably ask you what's
wrong, tell you

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:46.440
what's wrong, ask you for help.

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:48.410
It's one of the
things I appreciate,

00:30:48.410 --> 00:30:51.170
to an incredible degree
about the community here

00:30:51.170 --> 00:30:54.620
is that we're open to questions
that when people email

00:30:54.620 --> 00:30:57.350
you or tweet at you or
send you a GitHub request,

00:30:57.350 --> 00:30:59.690
you answer those questions
politely and kindly

00:30:59.690 --> 00:31:00.970
and with compassion.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:05.340
It means the world to people
who are just starting out.

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:06.900
And we also need
to be able to scale

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:08.670
employment and internships.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:11.572
Our original idea was
basically born out of panic.

00:31:11.572 --> 00:31:13.030
I hadn't really
thought through how

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:14.790
we were going to get people
jobs at the end of this training

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:15.940
program we started.

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:18.090
That's my fault.
We needed something

00:31:18.090 --> 00:31:20.340
a little better than just
starting a random consulting

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:21.450
company.

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:24.180
And so we need to be able
to scale those employment

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:26.972
opportunities and those
internship opportunities.

00:31:26.972 --> 00:31:29.430
And this is where partnerships
with organizations that know

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:31.350
what they're doing is critical.

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:33.930
So we've been working with
the Urban Alliance, who

00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:38.220
has a whole program and a whole
system for managing internships

00:31:38.220 --> 00:31:41.220
for young people who come from
communities like the one we're

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:42.870
trying to serve.

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:44.400
And so we're
partnering with them

00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:46.980
to be able to run these
internships in a long term

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:49.700
and sustainable way.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:51.560
We also need advocates.

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:53.227
So one of the things
that we've realized

00:31:53.227 --> 00:31:55.227
is we're going through
this program is the thing

00:31:55.227 --> 00:31:56.397
that Problem Forward did.

00:31:56.397 --> 00:31:58.730
It wasn't that it was a data
science consulting company.

00:31:58.730 --> 00:32:01.200
It wasn't that it was related
to the training program.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.535
It was that we could advocate
on behalf of young people

00:32:03.535 --> 00:32:05.660
who come out of these
communities, who are talented

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:08.030
individuals, who have
credentials that maybe look

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:10.820
a little different, who
maybe don't have all

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:13.340
of the background that other
people might have when they're

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:16.070
first starting out, but
that are really talented

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:18.710
and could be spectacular
if just given a chance.

00:32:18.710 --> 00:32:21.260
And so within all organizations,
we need those advocates,

00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:23.300
and those advocates have
to be people like you,

00:32:23.300 --> 00:32:26.347
people like me, people who
have opportunity already

00:32:26.347 --> 00:32:28.430
and can reach out and try
to support somebody else

00:32:28.430 --> 00:32:29.720
as they go through
that same journey

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:32.262
that we all went through that
I had to go through when I came

00:32:32.262 --> 00:32:35.170
from Idaho to get to here.

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:37.880
And the other thing we need is
mutually intensive learning.

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:39.520
It's been an
incredible experience,

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:41.392
I think, for both
sides, both people

00:32:41.392 --> 00:32:42.850
that are coming
out of the training

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:46.120
program and the mentors and
teachers and hiring partners

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.070
to learn about each
other, to learn

00:32:48.070 --> 00:32:49.490
about the things we do well.

00:32:49.490 --> 00:32:52.017
You know, I don't know the
first thing about Instagram

00:32:52.017 --> 00:32:54.350
and TikTok and all that kind
of stuff, but I'm learning.

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:55.147
I'm learning.

00:32:55.147 --> 00:32:57.230
And that's how we're getting
into new communities.

00:32:57.230 --> 00:32:58.980
And I'm learning that
from the people that

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:00.380
are in the training program.

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:02.950
So I think that
there's a way that we

00:33:02.950 --> 00:33:04.570
can learn from each other.

00:33:04.570 --> 00:33:06.790
It's not just teaching data
science in one direction,

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:09.110
it's also learning in the
other direction as well.

00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:10.990
And that mutually
intensive learning

00:33:10.990 --> 00:33:12.700
can really develop
a relationship

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:16.070
that lasts a long time.

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:17.077
So what's the future?

00:33:17.077 --> 00:33:18.410
What's the future of DataTrail?

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:19.650
I told you about the pilot.

00:33:19.650 --> 00:33:20.942
I told you about where we were.

00:33:20.942 --> 00:33:22.760
We're in a great place
with DataTrail now,

00:33:22.760 --> 00:33:24.802
and we're just starting
to think about the future

00:33:24.802 --> 00:33:27.100
and how do we scale this up?

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:30.840
So our DataTrail model has
three big components now.

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:34.180
There's colleges and
universities or training

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:35.570
partners in general.

00:33:35.570 --> 00:33:38.770
They provide tutors and
educational support.

00:33:38.770 --> 00:33:40.985
Then there's hiring
partners or mentors.

00:33:40.985 --> 00:33:43.360
They provide the mentors and
opportunities for the people

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:45.460
once they finish the
training program.

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:47.320
And then we have
nonprofit and community

00:33:47.320 --> 00:33:49.600
based partners who
do the hard work

00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:51.400
of supporting the whole person.

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:53.740
Because when you're
doing a training program

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:55.907
and you're coming from a
community that doesn't have

00:33:55.907 --> 00:33:58.905
a lot of resources, small
things can cause big problems

00:33:58.905 --> 00:34:00.280
if you don't have
the support you

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:02.550
need to get through
the training program.

00:34:02.550 --> 00:34:04.810
And so the three
groups working together

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:07.720
can really provide that
infrastructure, that underlying

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:10.270
infrastructure that gives
somebody the opportunity

00:34:10.270 --> 00:34:12.760
to come from a community
without a lot of opportunity

00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:16.429
into a place where they can
be a leader in the field.

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:19.850
So in Baltimore, we do this with
the Johns Hopkins data science

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:22.070
lab, providing the
tutoring and support.

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:24.192
So we've got Candice
Savonen in here

00:34:24.192 --> 00:34:26.400
who's from the, was at the
Johns Hopkins data science,

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:28.500
and that lab is now at the
Fred Hutch data science lab.

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:30.350
I don't know where she is,
but she could stand up.

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:32.058
But she's helping to
provide the tutoring

00:34:32.058 --> 00:34:34.770
and support with Davon.

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:36.900
We have mentors
and opportunities

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:38.969
that we're building out
with Urban Alliance,

00:34:38.969 --> 00:34:42.150
and then we have incredible
nonprofit and community

00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:44.370
based partnerships
built in Baltimore

00:34:44.370 --> 00:34:47.159
that provide social and life
support to the young people who

00:34:47.159 --> 00:34:50.280
are going through our programs
so that if something comes up,

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:51.511
they can get that support.

00:34:51.511 --> 00:34:53.219
They need to stay in
the program to stick

00:34:53.219 --> 00:34:57.290
with it, to get the opportunity
that they're going after.

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:59.850
So this is how our
program works now.

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:02.650
From January to June, we're
recruiting young people.

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:06.820
We're getting into schools,
we're providing content online.

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:10.130
We're trying to get people
excited about data science.

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:12.350
And then from June
to September, we're

00:35:12.350 --> 00:35:13.770
doing our training program.

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:15.663
We're actually running
the DataTrail courses

00:35:15.663 --> 00:35:16.580
they're going through.

00:35:16.580 --> 00:35:18.622
They're getting paid as
they complete the courses

00:35:18.622 --> 00:35:20.360
and they're finishing
the program.

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:21.777
And we're also
recruiting mentors.

00:35:21.777 --> 00:35:23.735
We're recruiting the
people that are ultimately

00:35:23.735 --> 00:35:26.300
going to hire and mentor
and manage the young people

00:35:26.300 --> 00:35:28.500
in their first opportunity.

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:32.070
Then from September to
June, they do an internship.

00:35:32.070 --> 00:35:34.020
This is supported
by Urban Alliance

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:37.440
and this is a 10 hour a week
internship where they get paid,

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:40.080
it's virtual and they get
mentorship a couple of hours

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:44.130
a week from a mentor at a
hiring company or a partner.

00:35:44.130 --> 00:35:46.790
This is how the
program works now.

00:35:46.790 --> 00:35:48.800
And so there's an
opportunity for you

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:52.550
to get involved with us at
DataTrail, if you're interested.

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:54.380
There's two different ways.

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:59.270
One is the more heavy duty way,
which is start your own

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:01.250
DataTrail in your own community.

00:36:01.250 --> 00:36:04.490
And the other way is to hire
or mentor DataTrail graduates

00:36:04.490 --> 00:36:06.180
from our program.

00:36:06.180 --> 00:36:08.910
So I'm going to start with
starting your own DataTrail

00:36:08.910 --> 00:36:09.820
program.

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:12.120
We talked a little bit
about doing hard things

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:14.730
and we did a hard thing
to set this thing up,

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:17.448
and we've paved a lot
of the ground for you.

00:36:17.448 --> 00:36:19.740
So if you're interested in
starting your own DataTrail

00:36:19.740 --> 00:36:23.640
community, you can reach out
to Ashley Johnson, to myself,

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:25.950
and we can provide you with
a lot of the background

00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:29.370
and materials you need to build
this program in your community.

00:36:29.370 --> 00:36:32.460
We can provide start up
documents, relationship

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:33.450
agreements.

00:36:33.450 --> 00:36:35.780
We can give you information
on how to get funded.

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:39.120
We provide free courses so
you can access them and train

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:40.510
people in data science.

00:36:40.510 --> 00:36:43.120
And then we provide access
to the DataTrail community.

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:44.670
So you can talk
to the people who

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:48.400
are building it in Baltimore
and around the country.

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:51.700
I'm very excited
because the first DataTrail

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:54.040
franchise has started already.

00:36:54.040 --> 00:36:57.670
So Madhu and
Melanie contacted us.

00:36:57.670 --> 00:37:00.070
They're at Mount Sinai Health
System in New York city,

00:37:00.070 --> 00:37:03.250
and they were really
excited about DataTrail.

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:05.890
And the two of them set
up a DataTrail franchise

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:08.170
that's running completely
independently in New York

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:10.720
and is now on its second
cohort of graduates,

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:13.090
placing people in the
medical health system,

00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:15.130
placing them at jobs
around New York City.

00:37:15.130 --> 00:37:19.450
It's amazing to see how just
a couple of committed people

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:21.280
can have such an
incredible impact

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:23.390
on the lives of young
people in their community.

00:37:23.390 --> 00:37:24.880
And I'm so impressed
with the work

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:26.265
that Madhu and Melanie are doing.

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:31.278
Yeah, it's awesome.

00:37:34.420 --> 00:37:37.138
So the other way you can get
involved is to hire or mentor.

00:37:37.138 --> 00:37:38.680
So we're working
with Urban Alliance,

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:41.500
as I mentioned, and there's
two ways that you can do this.

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:45.790
You could hire DataTrail
graduates into your company

00:37:45.790 --> 00:37:48.160
if you have internships
that you want to support.

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:49.640
It's part time internships.

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:52.030
They're remote, paid
internships, and they

00:37:52.030 --> 00:37:54.470
can be supported by Urban
Alliance and DataTrail.

00:37:54.470 --> 00:37:57.130
In other words, we support
them on the technical side

00:37:57.130 --> 00:38:00.220
and Urban Alliance supports
them on the soft skills side.

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:02.080
I'm incredibly
excited to announce

00:38:02.080 --> 00:38:06.130
that Posit has committed to
host two of these interns

00:38:06.130 --> 00:38:08.140
this fall from our
DataTrail cohort, which

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:09.658
is an amazing opportunity.

00:38:09.658 --> 00:38:10.575
And thank you so much.

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.310
The other cool
opportunity and this

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:21.060
I'm also wildly excited about, is
that you can mentor DataTrail

00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:23.610
graduates as an open
source developer,

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:25.748
so we could place people
directly into companies.

00:38:25.748 --> 00:38:28.290
But there's also an incredible
opportunity for our DataTrail

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:30.930
grads to make an impact on the
open source and open science

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:31.980
community.

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:35.340
And so the idea here is we get
philanthropic and corporate

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:37.590
funding to pay for
the internship,

00:38:37.590 --> 00:38:41.700
and then we will recruit mentors
from the open source community

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:43.750
to work on their projects.

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:46.740
And so Posit again has
committed incredibly

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:48.550
to fund one of these this fall.

00:38:48.550 --> 00:38:50.412
If you're an open
source developer that's

00:38:50.412 --> 00:38:52.620
looking for somebody to work
with you on your project

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:54.660
and you're looking for
a talented young person,

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:57.570
you should come and talk
to us after the event.

00:38:57.570 --> 00:38:59.460
And then the Abell
Foundation is also

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:02.490
agreed to fund one more this
fall from our training program

00:39:02.490 --> 00:39:04.170
and that represents
our entire training

00:39:04.170 --> 00:39:06.030
cohort is going to
get funded this year

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:09.150
and we're excited about
building this over time.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:11.610
So if you have an
idea about a program

00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:16.020
that if you want to either
mentor a student this fall

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:19.750
or you want to start thinking
about next fall, get in touch.

00:39:19.750 --> 00:39:22.230
I've got the email addresses
up here of Chas Ackley,

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:24.600
who's the Urban Alliance
partner, the director

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:27.900
of the Baltimore office,
and Ashley Johnson, who's

00:39:27.900 --> 00:39:30.400
the director of the
DataTrail program in Baltimore.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:32.700
And you can reach out to
either of them or to me

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:35.150
if you're excited about
mentoring a DataTrail grad.

00:39:38.030 --> 00:39:40.400
I'd like to finish, though,
talking about not just Data

00:39:40.400 --> 00:39:42.260
Trail, because this
is a cool opportunity

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:44.360
and we would love to
work with all of you,

00:39:44.360 --> 00:39:46.907
but you can also do it in any
other way that you feel like.

00:39:46.907 --> 00:39:48.740
I think just bringing
it back to what I said

00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:50.555
at the beginning of the talk.

00:39:50.555 --> 00:39:51.930
This is an incredible
opportunity

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:54.310
to be together, to build
community together.

00:39:54.310 --> 00:39:56.610
And I think that what
Posit and RStudio have done

00:39:56.610 --> 00:39:59.220
is just an amazing way
to make us all feel

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:01.700
part of the same community.

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:05.095
One of the things that we
should do is help each other,

00:40:05.095 --> 00:40:06.470
whether that's
through DataTrail

00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:10.010
or through Openscapes
or R-Ladies or Data

00:40:10.010 --> 00:40:13.100
Carpentry or any other program
that you're excited about.

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:15.470
And there's this
quote that I love.

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:18.200
I emailed my undergrad
advisor when I first

00:40:18.200 --> 00:40:20.187
got my very first faculty
job, and I told him

00:40:20.187 --> 00:40:21.270
I was so excited about it.

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:24.080
I got to live near my wife,
I got to live in Baltimore,

00:40:24.080 --> 00:40:25.710
I got to do my dream job.

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:29.220
And he wrote me back that
he was really glad to hear.

00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:33.100
And he said, mentorship is
a debt you can't pay off.

00:40:33.100 --> 00:40:35.240
I had some mentors who
helped me when I was young

00:40:35.240 --> 00:40:37.050
and I can never
pay it off to them.

00:40:37.050 --> 00:40:39.260
I can only pay it
forward to other people.

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:42.680
And so he said, Thank you for
helping me pay off my debt.

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:44.305
And I feel like we're
all in this room.

00:40:44.305 --> 00:40:46.555
Owe a debt of gratitude to
all of the people that have

00:40:46.555 --> 00:40:47.970
helped us be here to get today.

00:40:47.970 --> 00:40:50.330
And whichever way you
choose to express that,

00:40:50.330 --> 00:40:53.000
I hope you'll think about
ways that you can pay off

00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:55.930
your or pay forward your debt.

00:40:55.930 --> 00:40:57.930
There's almost nothing
more rewarding than this.

00:40:57.930 --> 00:40:59.420
It's been an incredible journey.

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:01.990
And I think that the people
here today, the DataTrail

00:41:01.990 --> 00:41:07.540
folks, the Urban Alliance folks,
the Heartsmiles folks, the HEBCAC

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:09.343
folks, they're all
here to say that this

00:41:09.343 --> 00:41:11.260
is one of the most
rewarding things you can do

00:41:11.260 --> 00:41:14.210
is create opportunities
for young people.

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:14.880
With that,

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:15.720
I'll Thank you.

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:18.380
And at the conclusion of the
talk, after this is all over,

00:41:18.380 --> 00:41:21.320
I'm going to be hanging out
up here in front with Ashley,

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:23.177
with Chas, with
Davon, with Candice.

00:41:23.177 --> 00:41:25.010
If you're excited about
mentoring or talking

00:41:25.010 --> 00:41:27.135
about DataTrail, please
come and hang out and talk.

00:41:27.135 --> 00:41:29.030
And we'd love to
get to talk to you.

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:30.740
Thank you very much
for your attention.

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:44.500
Thanks, Jeff.

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:49.020
Now we engage in light
banter for, live banter,

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:52.440
for one to 2 minutes until
people ask questions and have

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:54.490
a chance to upvote them.

00:41:54.490 --> 00:41:56.790
So light banter about ggplot2?

00:41:56.790 --> 00:41:58.050
Yeah, sure.

00:41:58.050 --> 00:42:01.340
I'm glad I got my digs
in before the talk.

00:42:01.340 --> 00:42:02.140
Yeah, that was,

00:42:02.140 --> 00:42:03.353
that was a good investment.

00:42:03.353 --> 00:42:04.770
I feel like that's
the only reason

00:42:04.770 --> 00:42:06.210
I got the invitation
was so Hadley

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:10.050
got to say that. I was actually
reading through there's

00:42:10.050 --> 00:42:10.950
been a few.

00:42:10.950 --> 00:42:12.450
You also wrote one
on tidy data that

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:15.420
wasn't really like against
tidy data, but I was like,

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:19.170
no, that was very much like
these are some use cases

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:22.060
or it's like not great
for and yeah, it was.

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:22.700
Right Yeah.

00:42:22.700 --> 00:42:23.290
Oh, man.

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:24.415
I'm getting all the gripes.

00:42:24.415 --> 00:42:27.090
Somebody save me with
the question, please.

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:29.320
OK we do have one
question, one question,

00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:31.260
one just one to save you.

00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:34.300
Are the mentoring opportunities
only available in Baltimore?

00:42:34.300 --> 00:42:37.330
No, these are remote online
mentorship opportunities.

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:39.690
So if you are an
open source developer

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:42.375
and are excited about
mentoring a young person,

00:42:42.375 --> 00:42:44.500
it's going to get paid for
by the Abell foundation.

00:42:44.500 --> 00:42:46.083
It's going to get
paid for by Posit.

00:42:46.083 --> 00:42:48.120
So you just get
an awesome mentee

00:42:48.120 --> 00:42:49.660
to work with you for a year.

00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:51.780
So if you're an open
source developer anywhere,

00:42:51.780 --> 00:42:54.700
please reach out.

00:42:54.700 --> 00:43:00.220
And the next question,
how have the program graduates

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:01.720
been doing long term?

00:43:01.720 --> 00:43:02.570
How're things?

00:43:02.570 --> 00:43:04.910
Yeah, they're all
over the place.

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:07.882
They're doing everything
from data science jobs,

00:43:07.882 --> 00:43:09.340
but the kind of
cool thing about it

00:43:09.340 --> 00:43:11.685
is people like Davon
is working with us.

00:43:11.685 --> 00:43:13.060
We've got other
young people that

00:43:13.060 --> 00:43:14.768
are working in data
science jobs that, one

00:43:14.768 --> 00:43:19.250
has converted to python,
which is great, but, you know.

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:20.810
I won't say that too loudly.

00:43:20.810 --> 00:43:22.342
But then there's
also some people

00:43:22.342 --> 00:43:24.050
that just took the
training and then went

00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:26.030
in a different direction
in what they cared about.

00:43:26.030 --> 00:43:27.680
I think one of the cool
things about the program

00:43:27.680 --> 00:43:30.287
is we're catching young people
at a really formative time

00:43:30.287 --> 00:43:30.870
of their life.

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:33.180
So it's 17 to 23 or so.

00:43:33.180 --> 00:43:36.710
And I'm sure you all
remember you didn't exactly

00:43:36.710 --> 00:43:39.030
know what you wanted
to do when you were 17.

00:43:39.030 --> 00:43:41.150
And so data science
could be cool for a while

00:43:41.150 --> 00:43:42.170
and they learn
some stuff from us

00:43:42.170 --> 00:43:43.560
and then they maybe go apply it

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:45.455
so the skills they
learn somewhere else.

00:43:45.455 --> 00:43:48.080
And so we have some people that
end up in data science careers,

00:43:48.080 --> 00:43:49.610
some people that
go back to school,

00:43:49.610 --> 00:43:51.200
some people that
end up like doing

00:43:51.200 --> 00:43:52.700
something totally
different but kind

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:54.697
of leveraging the training.

00:43:54.697 --> 00:43:56.280
But we keep in touch
with all of them.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:58.730
And so I'm always
texting with people

00:43:58.730 --> 00:44:00.560
that have graduated
from the program

00:44:00.560 --> 00:44:03.630
and it's cool to see that
community build over time.

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:06.350
So what are some
of the challenges

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:09.020
you faced in terms of both
kind of like buy in and just

00:44:09.020 --> 00:44:11.460
like raising awareness
of the program?

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:14.040
Yeah so I think that
I tried to highlight

00:44:14.040 --> 00:44:16.440
a couple of the key issues,
but like there's, there's

00:44:16.440 --> 00:44:18.660
been a whole
collection of issues

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:22.080
in doing this, everything
from HR problems

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:24.360
to legal problems to
bureaucratic problems

00:44:24.360 --> 00:44:27.040
to building data science
program problems.

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:29.310
And I think the core
message, though,

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:32.970
is that the thing
that helps most

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:35.590
is to think about the
target at the end.

00:44:35.590 --> 00:44:38.490
So our goal is to help young
people get opportunities.

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:39.280
Sometimes

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:41.160
that's by building a
data science program,

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:44.460
but sometimes it's by Ashley and
I going to a million HR meetings

00:44:44.460 --> 00:44:46.450
to try to create a new job code.

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:48.930
And so I think thinking about
that target, like what you're

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:51.960
trying to create at the end,
gives you a lot of leeway

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:54.030
to do all the things
you need to do

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:56.500
and then being flexible
about learning new things.

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:58.750
I think that's been the hard
thing for me, definitely.

00:44:58.750 --> 00:45:00.120
I didn't know the
first thing about,

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:01.570
you know, I'm a biostatistician.

00:45:01.570 --> 00:45:02.945
What do I know
about any of that?

00:45:02.945 --> 00:45:04.240
But I'm learning over time.

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:07.610
And I feel like having smart
people I work with helps.

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:10.760
What about plans as you
transition to The Hutch?

00:45:10.760 --> 00:45:12.350
Yeah so we're
getting ready to be

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:13.890
able to run the
program in Seattle.

00:45:13.890 --> 00:45:15.830
So I'm excited about that.

00:45:15.830 --> 00:45:17.720
And then it's
running in New York.

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:19.510
We have a couple
of other ambitions

00:45:19.510 --> 00:45:21.260
about starting in a
couple of other cities

00:45:21.260 --> 00:45:24.420
I can't announce quite yet, but
that we're going to start soon.

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:26.450
And so really now we're
at the stage where we're

00:45:26.450 --> 00:45:27.840
excited about scaling it out.

00:45:27.840 --> 00:45:30.182
And so it's been running in
Baltimore for a few years.

00:45:30.182 --> 00:45:31.640
It's successful in
Baltimore, and I

00:45:31.640 --> 00:45:33.098
think it's the kind
of program that

00:45:33.098 --> 00:45:36.108
can very easily run in a
number of cities around the US

00:45:36.108 --> 00:45:36.900
that I highlighted.

00:45:36.900 --> 00:45:38.497
And so we're really
in this phase

00:45:38.497 --> 00:45:40.580
of trying to get connected
to the right people who

00:45:40.580 --> 00:45:42.390
are excited about
running those programs, so.

00:45:42.390 --> 00:45:43.260
Awesome.

00:45:43.260 --> 00:45:44.030
Thanks, Jeff.

00:45:44.030 --> 00:45:47.240
Really and that was like
just amazing, uplifting keynote.

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:48.600
So thank you so much.

00:45:48.600 --> 00:45:50.430
Appreciate it.

